-- Deliberately ONE item per order (not two): fetchOwnOrders()'s SQL does
-- `GROUP_CONCAT(ti.product_name SEPARATOR ', ')` with NO inner `ORDER BY` —
-- a genuine, pre-existing MySQL non-determinism (concatenation order across
-- multiple rows in one group is unspecified without one), faithfully
-- inherited by the Next port's identical SQL. Verified empirically: with two
-- items, PHP and Next occasionally returned the two product names in
-- different orders even though the underlying data was byte-identical — not
-- a port bug, just GROUP_CONCAT's own documented behavior. One item per
-- order sidesteps the ambiguity entirely rather than allowlisting `products`
-- and weakening the check.
